**Read:** [Convecção e métodos numéricos — Maliska, UFSC](../sources/Conveccao-and-Numerical-Methods-Maliska-UFSC.lingua#r_71K7JMRA8M0N3Y8EKMZJT9NGQ7), both complete available graduate course progressions; [A Heat Transfer Textbook — Lienhard and Lienhard](../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD), full coupled physical models; [FiPy — conservative finite-volume modelling](../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q), conservation and discretization principles; [Convective Heat Transfer — Arup Kumar Das](../sources/Convective-Heat-Transfer-Das.lingua#r_1BYB2QY61ENBK9WGCHBBC5YE92), complete relevant convection development.

Construct a coupled solid/fluid thermal problem from a physical service, with documented geometry, flow, material properties and heat generation. Start with separate analytical limits before joining the regions. Enforce interface energy conservation and the actual contact condition.

Follow complete applicable numerical/convection courses. Document mesh treatment, pressure/velocity coupling, discretization, turbulence or laminar assumptions, iterative convergence and discretization uncertainty. A low residual alone is not a grid-convergence or validation result.

Compare with independent measurements or a well-documented benchmark over changed flow and load. Examine how inlet turbulence, roughness, outlet placement, contact and radiation alter conclusions. Deliver a reduced model that can be audited alongside the resolved one, and show where the reduced approximation ceases to support the intended decision.

**Continue:** [A complete thermal service from physics to continuing use](A-complete-thermal-service-from-physics-to-continuing-use.lingua#r_7J8HFJ2KFHGSRF5EB4JFNRZRB2).

